 ],
 "literal": "Carpenter-made small horse-chariot / riding [it] they do not get pleasure, even though, with [their] hand / pulling [it] they get pleasure, children like, / experiencing [him] we do not get pleasure, even though, good chariot / pond-town chief's friendship / making [it], [my] heart rejoiced, [and] became tight, the bangles indeed.",
 "translation": "Like young children who, though they cannot ride and enjoy the small toy horse-chariot made by a carpenter, still find joy in dragging it with their hands, we too, though we do not get to embrace and enjoy him, find joy in merely having the friendship of the chief of the pond-filled town with fine chariots; and because my heart rejoiced in this, my bangles have become tight again.",
 "modern": "தச்சன் செய்த சிறிய குதிரை பொம்மை வண்டியை ஏறி ஓட்டி இன்பம் அடையாவிட்டாலும், கைகளால் அதனை இழுத்து விளையாடி இன்பம் அடையும் சிறு பிள்ளைகளைப் போல, நல்ல தேரையும் பொய்கைகளையும் உடைய ஊர்த் தலைவனுடைய நட்பை நாம் நேரில் கூடி இன்புறாவிட்டாலும், அவனது நட்பைக் கொண்டுள்ளோம் என்று நினைத்து என் நெஞ்சம் இன்புற்றது; அதனால் என் வளையல்கள் கழலாமல் இறுக்கமாகிவிட்டன.",
 "explanation": "The companion speaks about how the heroine sustains herself during separation from the hero. She compares the heroine's love to children playing with a toy chariot. Even though children cannot actually ride the toy chariot made by a carpenter, they derive immense joy just by dragging it around with their hands. Similarly, even though the heroine cannot physically embrace the chief of the agricultural tract who has fine chariots and ponds, she finds deep joy and sustenance just by thinking of his friendship. Because her heart rejoiced in this thought of his friendship, her body, which had grown thin in separation, has regained its health, and her bangles have become tight again. This imagery beautifully captures the Marutam landscape and the emotional state of the heroine, showing how even the mere thought of her lover's friendship sustains her life and restores her physical beauty.",
